    "body": "Ports America has appointed Craig Mygatt as its next chief executive, bringing a veteran of marine terminals, container shipping and infrastructure investment to the helm of the largest U.S. marine terminal operator and stevedore.\nThe Morristown, N.J.-based company said Mygatt will succeed Scott Anderson, who has served as interim CEO since July 2026 and will remain chairman of the board.\nCraig Mygatt\nMygatt brings more than 35 years of industry experience and most recently served as an operating partner at Macquarie Asset Management ( MQG.AX ). He previously led Ceres Terminals from 2020 to 2023, overseeing a North American terminal and stevedoring business spanning containers, cruise, roll-on/roll-off and breakbulk operations.\nBefore Ceres, Mygatt spent more than three decades at Maersk (OTC: AMKBY ) in roles including corporate planning and North American inland operations. He became CEO of SeaLand in 2014, with responsibility for Maersk Line’s operations across North American and Latin American trade lanes.\n“Craig is a proven leader with deep experience on both the terminal and the carrier sides of our industry,” Anderson said in a release. “His track record of safe, reliable operations and strong customer relationships makes him the right leader for Ports America’s next chapter.”\nPorts America operates at more than 70 locations across more than 30 U.S. ports, providing container, roll-on/roll-off, breakbulk, military and cruise services.\nThe company handled approximately 19 million twenty-foot equivalent units in 2024. By comparison, Drewry’s latest global review places PSA International first with 69.9 million TEUs in equity-adjusted throughput in 2025.\nMygatt said his priorities would center on the company’s people and operating relationships. “I look forward to working alongside our team, customers, labor partners and port authorities to build on that foundation, with safety and service at the center of everything we do,” he said.\nMygatt will take over as Ports America advances major development initiatives on the Gulf and East coasts. The company and Geneva-based Mediterranean Shipping Co.’s Terminal Investment Limited are private-sector partners in the Louisiana International Terminal, which received its federal construction permit in August. In Baltimore, Ports America Chesapeake’s Seagirt Marine Terminal is preparing for additional double-stack rail traffic through a $42 million intermodal expansion scheduled to begin construction in October and reach completion in 2028.\nA source told FreightWaves that Ports America has been looking at expansion overseas including in Panama, where longtime operator CK Hutchison ( 0001.HK ) of Hong Kong was recently ousted. Maersk’s APM Terminals received the temporary operating assignment for Balboa, on the Pacific side of the Panama Canal. MSC’s TIL unit received the corresponding assignment for Cristobal, on the Atlantic side. Both arrangements were approved for up to 18 months in February 2026.\nWhy It Matters: The appointment puts an executive with experience on both sides of the ship-to-shore relationship—ocean carriers and terminal operators—in charge of a major U.S. cargo-handling network.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nSavannah handles 565,366 TEUs in October, up 25% year-over-year on strong import demand\nAsia–US East Coast ocean freight rates top $10,000, up 300% from a year ago\nTentative union deal averts strike at Port of LA-Long Beach\nZim Q3 profit surges to $830 million as freight rates jump 55%\nShip pilot indicted after fatal Houston collision linked to cellphone distraction",
